{"summary":"Accept the chemistry-stress amendment because the probe's documented behavior and enforced assertions are currently inconsistent; reject the carbonate-equilibrium throttling amendment because the computation is already trace-only, appears cheap, and reducing its cadence would degrade per-tick trace fidelity without evidence of a real performance problem.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-1-4ed757e6","decision":"accept","rationale":"This is a real correctness and maintainability issue. The chemistry-stress probe still documents low-GH plus high-NO2 as producing molt failures, and the helper still computes `molt_failures`, but the current assertions no longer fail when both `molt_failures == 0` and `failed_molt_accum == 0`. That means the scenario can pass while never demonstrating the failed-molt outcome it claims to verify. Restoring a directional failed-molt assertion, or weakening the prose if the model no longer guarantees that behavior, would bring the probe back into alignment with its stated contract."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-f7d31042","decision":"reject","rationale":"The proposed throttling is not well justified by the implementation. `emit_tick_snapshot` only emits notes when stage tracing is enabled at `Verbosity::Trace`, so this work is already confined to the highest-detail diagnostic mode. The carbonate equilibrium path is a closed-form quadratic solve, not an iterative heavy routine, so there is no concrete evidence here of a material bottleneck. Computing it less often would also make `system:tick_snapshot` less faithful per tick and weaken the current trace surface, which explicitly includes `tick_snapshot.water.ph` in trace-mode tests."}]}